$article=['date'=>'2027-01-23','slug'=>'exam-stress-vs-exam-anxiety','tag'=>'Teen Mental Health','category'=>'Teen Mental Health','breadcrumb'=>'Exam Stress vs Anxiety','meta_title'=>'Exam Stress vs Anxiety: When Your Teen Needs More Support','description'=>'Learn how exam stress differs from exam anxiety, which changes deserve attention, and how parents can support a teenager without adding more pressure.','h1'=>'Exam Stress vs Exam Anxiety: When Normal Pressure Becomes a Mental Health Concern','dek'=>'The key question is not whether your teen feels nervous. It is whether anxiety is preventing sleep, study, attendance or ordinary life.','body'=><<<'HTML'
<p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-light">Exam stress is a common response to meaningful demands and often eases with preparation and recovery. Exam anxiety becomes more concerning when fear feels disproportionate, persists, causes strong physical symptoms or drives avoidance that interferes with study and functioning.</p><h2>Watch functioning, not marks alone</h2><p>Notice sleep, meals, concentration, school attendance, panic, reassurance-seeking and withdrawal. A high-scoring student can be significantly distressed, while disappointment about one result does not by itself indicate a disorder.</p><h2>Respond without adding pressure</h2><p>Ask what feels hardest and listen before offering a timetable. Break work into realistic steps, protect sleep and avoid comparisons, threats or repeated checking. Communicate that wellbeing is not conditional on results.</p><h2>When to seek help</h2><p>Seek professional support when anxiety persists, avoidance grows, physical symptoms recur or your teen cannot manage daily responsibilities. Immediate help is needed for self-harm, suicidal thoughts or inability to stay safe.</p><h2>What therapy can address</h2><p>Support may examine anxious predictions, avoidance, study habits, perfectionism, family communication and coping skills. Treatment should fit the teen rather than becoming another performance demand. Explore <a href="/teen-mental-health/exam-anxiety">exam anxiety support</a>.</p>
HTML,'faqs'=>[['Is exam anxiety a real mental health concern?','Yes. Exam anxiety can significantly affect sleep, concentration, physical wellbeing, attendance and performance. Severity and impairment matter more than whether exams are a normal stressor.'],['Should parents reduce all academic expectations?','Not automatically. Use realistic expectations, collaborative planning and protected recovery while addressing anxiety. Support should reduce harmful pressure without communicating that the teenager is incapable.'],['Can anxiety cause stomach aches before exams?','Yes, anxiety can cause physical symptoms. Recurrent, severe or unexplained symptoms should still receive appropriate medical review rather than being assumed to be psychological.'],['When is urgent help needed?','Seek immediate help if your teenager talks about suicide, self-harms, cannot stay safe or experiences a mental health emergency. Use local emergency services.']],
